WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.800
A water-restricted mouse starts to drink.

2
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.880
Within a minute, a population of its thirst neurons goes quiet.

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.780
Yet five minutes after water access, the salt in its blood is still raised.

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.260
If the brain only corrected errors it had measured in the blood, that could not

5
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.620
happen. So what is it responding to?

6
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:24.713
A simple loop model has four parts.

7
00:00:25.193 --> 00:00:29.053
The regulated variable is what is held steady, here plasma osmolality.

8
00:00:29.693 --> 00:00:33.653
A sensor measures it: osmosensitive neurons in the lamina terminalis.

9
00:00:34.193 --> 00:00:36.953
A comparator decides how far it is from where it should be.

10
00:00:37.213 --> 00:00:38.553
An effector pushes back.

11
00:00:39.313 --> 00:00:42.393
Vasopressin, or AVP, saves water at the kidney.

12
00:00:43.193 --> 00:00:44.233
Thirst takes water in.

13
00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:47.913
The response opposes the error, so this is negative feedback.

14
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:53.997
A hormone loop has a threshold, where the response starts, and a gain, how much it

15
00:00:53.997 --> 00:00:56.057
grows per unit of error above threshold.

16
00:00:56.797 --> 00:01:02.097
For healthy adults, one summary gives a threshold of 284 mOsm/kg.

17
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:07.417
The gain is 0.43 pmol/L for each mOsm/kg.

18
00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:09.797
Treat both as illustrative averages.

19
00:01:09.797 --> 00:01:13.837
Below threshold, the line predicts no osmotic release.

20
00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.390
Take a hypothetical adult on that line, at steady state.

21
00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.130
Osmolality is 294, ten above threshold.

22
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.550
Vasopressin is 4.30.

23
00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:30.270
After drinking, osmolality is 290, and vasopressin is 2.58.

24
00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.490
It fell by 1.72. Less error, less hormone.

25
00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.870
A pure feedback loop reacts to an error it has measured.

26
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:43.553
In water-restricted mice, the neurons' calcium signal falls within a minute of

27
00:01:43.553 --> 00:01:48.393
drinking. Seeing water without being allowed to drink does not lower their activity

28
00:01:48.393 --> 00:01:49.753
in salt-challenged mice.

29
00:01:50.593 --> 00:01:52.393
Licking an empty bottle does not either.

30
00:01:52.813 --> 00:01:56.853
So something that comes with drinking turns them down, ahead of any change in the

31
00:01:56.853 --> 00:02:01.573
blood. That is feedforward control, a response to a disturbance that is expected.

32
00:02:02.717 --> 00:02:05.137
Researchers put water straight into the stomach.

33
00:02:05.737 --> 00:02:10.857
In hydrated mice, it inhibited the neurons after about 105 seconds, before any

34
00:02:10.857 --> 00:02:13.157
detectable change in blood osmolarity.

35
00:02:13.737 --> 00:02:18.537
Keep the limits. This is a population calcium signal in mice, not the feeling of

36
00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:21.197
thirst. Feedback reacts to a measured error.

37
00:02:21.857 --> 00:02:23.797
Feedforward acts on an expected one.

38
00:02:24.437 --> 00:02:25.337
Thirst uses both.
